qa(None, "How does Bayes' theorem produce a classifier here?",
   f"For each phase k the model has a prior pi_k (how common the phase is) and a likelihood p(x | k) (how typical this pair of hormone values is for that phase), modelled as a bell-shaped Gaussian cloud estimated from the training women. Bayes' theorem multiplies them and normalises: p(k | x) = pi_k p(x | k) / sum_j pi_j p(x | j). The predicted phase is the one with the largest posterior. The class means, covariances and priors come from the training fold only. My implementation reproduces scikit-learn's QDA posteriors to {val(E3K, dict(sample='pooled'), 'max_abs_posterior_diff', '{:.1e}', label='E3 max |posterior diff| vs sklearn QDA')}.")
qa(None, "What is the difference between full, shared and diagonal covariance (QDA, LDA, naive Bayes)?",
   f"Full: every class has its own covariance matrix, which gives curved (quadratic) boundaries. Shared: one pooled covariance, so the boundaries are straight lines (LDA). Diagonal: no correlation between the two hormones inside a class (naive Bayes). More flexibility needs more parameters from the same training women. In my data the three give similar balanced accuracy within each prior setting (for example pooled, equal priors: full {val(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_equal'), 'bal_acc', '{:.3f}', label='E3 bal acc pooled full equal')}, shared {val(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_shared_equal'), 'bal_acc', '{:.3f}', label='E3 bal acc pooled shared equal')}, diagonal {val(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_diag_equal'), 'bal_acc', '{:.3f}', label='E3 bal acc pooled diag equal')}; {npw}).")
qa(None, "Why do empirical and equal priors give different balanced accuracies?",
   f"Empirical priors reflect how often each phase occurs in the training data, so the classifier favours common phases. That maximises plain accuracy but hurts the smallest class: the peri-ovulatory recall (pooled) was {val(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_empirical'), 'recall_peri_ov', '{:.2f}', label='E3 recall periov primary pooled')} with empirical priors and {val(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_equal'), 'recall_peri_ov', '{:.2f}', label='E3 recall periov equal pooled')} with equal priors. Balanced accuracy is the mean of per-class recalls, which is what equal priors optimise. Balanced accuracy was {ci(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_empirical'), 'bal_acc', '{:.3f}', lo='bal_acc_lo', hi='bal_acc_hi', label='E3 primary pooled')} versus {ci(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_equal'), 'bal_acc', '{:.3f}', lo='bal_acc_lo', hi='bal_acc_hi', label='E3 equal pooled')} ({npw}).")
qa(None, "How does the Gaussian Bayes classifier compare with logistic regression?",
   f"Both are in the same family (LDA's posterior is a logistic function; QDA adds quadratic terms) but they are fitted differently: logistic regression fits the boundary directly, the Bayes classifier models how the hormones are distributed in each phase. On these data the class-balanced version (equal priors) is not clearly different from logistic regression: difference in balanced accuracy {ci(E3M, dict(sample='pooled', model='gauss_bayes_full_equal'), 'd_bal_acc_vs_logreg', '{:+.3f}', lo='d_lo', hi='d_hi', label='E3 diff equal pooled')} pooled ({npw}) and {ci(E3M, dict(sample='long', model='gauss_bayes_full_equal'), 'd_bal_acc_vs_logreg', '{:+.3f}', lo='d_lo', hi='d_hi', label='E3 diff equal long')} longitudinal ({nlw}); the version with empirical priors is lower, for the reason above. The Bayes classifier has the advantage of producing interpretable class-wise parameters and posterior probabilities.")
